The important quantity is not an absolute zero of energy but the change produced when the separation changes. Denote the vacuum energy per unit area by $\mathcal E(d)=E(d)/A$. Once $\mathcal E$ depends on $d$, it is a potential energy, and the force per unit area is $\frac{F}{A}=-\frac{d\mathcal E}{dd}.$ Thus a rearrangement of virtual processes becomes an observable force. In the familiar electromagnetic example with ideal conducting plates, the energy and pressure are $\begin{aligned}
\mathcal E(d)&=-\frac{\pi^2}{720\,d^3},\\
\frac{F}{A}&=-\frac{\pi^2}{240\,d^4},\end{aligned}$ in units $\hbar=c=1$. The minus sign denotes attraction.

